+# Filtering the Veterinarian Directory by Specialty Is In Scope; Veterinarian Search Stays Out
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+NG-9 declined search for pets, visits, and veterinarians in one row, on the reason that owner search already demonstrates paged prefix search once. The row was derived from observed absence during the 2026-07-31 bootstrap survey, so its provenance stayed open.
+
+A request has now arrived for filtering the veterinarian directory to a single specialty, reached by a URL parameter on both directory surfaces. The row as written would decline it, because it reads "searching for a veterinarian" as covering any narrowing of that list. That reading also covers narrowing by an attribute the directory already prints on the page, which is a different capability from free-text search: it takes an exact value from a fixed, seeded list rather than a fragment of user-typed text, and it repeats no part of the owner-search pattern.
+
+The same request reinstates the machine-readable veterinarian list. That surface was withdrawn to the Superseded list on 2026-07-31 as an implementation artifact with no consumer. The filter is the first capability anyone has asked it for, which supplies the consumer the withdrawal found missing.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Leave NG-9 whole and decline the request.** Rejected: the row's reason does not reach attribute filtering. Declining on it would extend a boundary past what it was decided for.
+2. **Retire NG-9 entirely.** Rejected: free-text veterinarian search still repeats the owner-search pattern and still teaches nothing new. Only the attribute-filter case has a reason to be in.
+3. **Narrow NG-9 and reinstate the machine-readable list under a fresh requirement id** (chosen).
+
+## Decision
+
+NG-9 narrows. Free-text search for a pet, a visit, or a veterinarian remains out of scope. Filtering the veterinarian directory by an attribute it already displays is in scope, and specialty is the attribute.
+
+The machine-readable veterinarian list becomes a supported surface under `REQ-VET-003`. `REQ-VET-002` stays withdrawn and its id is never reused, so links to the withdrawal still resolve to the withdrawal.
+
+No visible page control accompanies this decision. The filter is reached by URL only, on both surfaces; a control is a separate request that has not been made.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- NG-9 leaves the open provenance consultation for the veterinarian clause; the pet and visit clauses stay in it, undecided.
+- The machine-readable list acquires a consumer contract. It can no longer be removed as an artifact, which the withdrawal had left open as a possibility.
+- The directory grows a second reading of "what the list contains" — filtered and unfiltered — that every future directory capability has to hold.
+- Widening the filter to another attribute, or to free text, is a recorded owner decision with its own non-goal ADR, per the Non-Goals table convention.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Non-goal:** NG-9
+
+- [PRD Non-Goals](../prd.md#non-goals) — the narrowed row.
+- [PRD Veterinarian directory](../prd.md#req-vet-003) — the reinstated machine-readable surface and the specialty filter.
